      "message": "[Review from Jetski] Previously `OnResolvedWithoutChecks` only ran `execute_rule_impl` when `rust_target_` was already populated (i.e. Starlark-defined targets), whereas calling `rust_target(session)` here now registers and evaluates `run_builtin_rule` for *every* builtin C++ target whenever `has_starlark_session()` is true.\n\nTwo questions/nits:\n1. In large builds with Starlark enabled, registering every C++ target under `Session::targets.write()` and allocating/freezing a Starlark heap per target could cause lock contention and noticeable CPU/memory overhead — is eager evaluation for all builtin targets intentional here, or should it be documented/deferred?\n2. The comment in `src/gn/target.h` (line 111: \"if it has one\") and the commit message still describe this as only running custom Starlark rule implementations; consider clarifying that this also evaluates builtin rules to populate providers.",
